Z||000000||Fiero Fanatic||12-31-2000||04:02 AM||||I was having a really nice day...Until My 1984 Fireo had walking miles on the freeway to call a tow truck. I'm thinking "What the f**k". <BR>Here's some history about the car......<P>(1) Recent Tune-up performed 2 months ago.(Replaced plugs,cap,rotor,fuel/air filter,plug wires,oil change,and set timing).<BR>(2) fairley new 02 sensor.<BR>(which since new has a code 44 problem below 50 mph).<BR>(3) New oil pump and oil shaft.<BR>(oil sprocket broke off the oil shaft recently).<BR>(4) Clutch 1 year old. <BR>(Did it in 3 hrs...including fork..no joke!).<BR>(5) The occasional starter re-built (I'll be a college major soon).<P>Except for the O2 sensor...which I exhausted all possible remedies except replacing with another new one. And, I've been using a smaller Ford Fiesta radiator in place of that stock GM plastic radiator. Because of the smaller size, I took out the themostat.<BR>It runs about 120-130 degrees.<P>It has been very a relaible car...except the other day. Upon having it towed from the freeway back to my house, I preceeded to commence on a search and repair mission. All The engine wants to do is crank over and nothing more. Not even a hint of wanting to fire right up.<P>My only finding is there's no conical fuel spray from the injector when cranking it over. It tried starting fluid and still it just cranks over and doesn't attempt to start. The battery is charged. There is spark from the coil to the plugs. Dispite the code 44 stored in memory, there is no new codes. The fuel relay works, and I hear the fuel pump run (2 sec.) building pressure. It has fuel in the tank, and all power and fuses and accessories operate like normal.<P>So I'll review...Air,Fuel,Spark = Nothing.<BR>Say if there wasn't fuel pressure? Why wouldn't it attempt to start from starting fluid? If the ECM gone bad? Why wouldn't I get a code 55? or any any other code relating to the problem? Any suggestions please help cause I'm a frantic Fiero Fanatic.<BR><P>------------------<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||tiacPaul||12-31-2000||04:24 AM||||Until i got to the end i was gonna ask if it had gas in it, but since you answered that,  i think i sounds like the fuel pump maybe the problem of a clogged filter.  Remember there are two, one in line and another on the fuel pump itself.  Good Luck.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fiero Fanatic||12-31-2000||07:43 AM||||Just climbed out of the forum archives. And before everyone asks if I replaced the ignition module the answer is "No". Didn't realize they can cause so many Fiero's to up and die. Sounds like replacing this along with a pick-up coil may be the answer. If it works? I'll will be joing an elite club of "ignition module killed the Fiero club".<BR>I'll keep everyone posted on that  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||3
